44
00:02:02,530 --> 00:02:05,860
Now it seems like transformers
are in fashion all the time.

45
00:02:05,860 --> 00:02:08,830
So it seems to be solving almost
every single problem which

46
00:02:08,830 --> 00:02:12,620
is being thrown at them.

47
00:02:12,620 --> 00:02:15,040
So what's special
about transformers?

48
00:02:15,040 --> 00:02:19,180
One of the fact that struck
me was their simplicity which

49
00:02:19,180 --> 00:02:23,710
is if you think about it--

50
00:02:23,710 --> 00:02:25,690
and it has been
hugely popular also.

51
00:02:25,690 --> 00:02:29,500
So it was just released in
2018 and within three years,

52
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:31,510
it has about 30,000
citations and it

53
00:02:31,510 --> 00:02:35,350
is kind of solving every single
problem in every single domain.

54
00:02:35,350 --> 00:02:38,600
It has its limitations
though, also.

55
00:02:38,600 --> 00:02:40,720
But if you think
about it, in a way,

56
00:02:40,720 --> 00:02:42,910
transformers are
basically like a way

57
00:02:42,910 --> 00:02:47,060
of just cascading self-attention
with feature learning.

58
00:02:47,060 --> 00:02:49,180
And if we keep on doing
it over and over again,

59
00:02:49,180 --> 00:02:52,180
then the model in a way learns
which parts of the input

60
00:02:52,180 --> 00:02:55,690
are important and keep on
transforming them, removing

61
00:02:55,690 --> 00:02:57,340
the content which
are not important,

62
00:02:57,340 --> 00:02:59,980
and just have the
limited information which

63
00:02:59,980 --> 00:03:04,180
is just responsible
for a particular task.

64
00:03:04,180 --> 00:03:05,680
And it has been
very, very difficult

65
00:03:05,680 --> 00:03:09,070
to keep up with the literature.

66
00:03:09,070 --> 00:03:11,440
I have put it as a
joke here, but then

67
00:03:11,440 --> 00:03:13,840
even Twitter's
recommendation engine would

68
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:16,420
kind of just getting out of--

69
00:03:16,420 --> 00:03:19,450
they were getting haywire as
to why is Chris Manning just

70
00:03:19,450 --> 00:03:21,550
searching over transformers.

71
00:03:21,550 --> 00:03:24,430
And that was way back in 2020.

72
00:03:24,430 --> 00:03:26,530
So it has been difficult
for researchers

73
00:03:26,530 --> 00:03:29,830
also to keep up with the
pace of what's going on.

74
00:03:29,830 --> 00:03:33,070
Just before transformers,
all of the NLP community

75
00:03:33,070 --> 00:03:35,830
was just going gaga
about bidirectional LSTMs

76
00:03:35,830 --> 00:03:36,740
with attention.

77
00:03:36,740 --> 00:03:38,920
So every single
paper before 2017

78
00:03:38,920 --> 00:03:42,340
was just like you have
encoder and LSTM layers.

79
00:03:42,340 --> 00:03:45,940
You keep on adding
multiple layers.

80
00:03:45,940 --> 00:03:48,430
And then after that, you
have attention mechanism

81
00:03:48,430 --> 00:03:50,720
which just learns
at what's important

82
00:03:50,720 --> 00:03:54,700
and then just keeps on decoding
sequentially one at a time.

83
00:03:54,700 --> 00:03:58,030
But this was not kind of
like an ideal way to do it

84
00:03:58,030 --> 00:04:01,570
because what turns out is
when we start throwing longer

85
00:04:01,570 --> 00:04:07,090
sequences, the
connections are no longer

86
00:04:07,090 --> 00:04:10,030
storing the gradient updates
in a way it should be doing.

87
00:04:10,030 --> 00:04:13,390
So what the researchers
from Google said--

88
00:04:13,390 --> 00:04:15,640
instead of having
just a attention layer

89
00:04:15,640 --> 00:04:20,680
at the very last encoding, we
would just have these attention

90
00:04:20,680 --> 00:04:23,620
mechanisms at every single
layer which, in a way,

91
00:04:23,620 --> 00:04:25,840
would just learn what's
important for a particular

92
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:28,600
problem at that
particular layer and we

93
00:04:28,600 --> 00:04:32,290
keep on doing it
over and over again.

94
00:04:32,290 --> 00:04:35,920
So then the whole idea of
transformers and attention

95
00:04:35,920 --> 00:04:39,452
mechanism cascaded one
after the other came.

96
00:04:39,452 --> 00:04:41,410
And I love going to the
details because this is

97
00:04:41,410 --> 00:04:43,390
the last class of the course.

98
00:04:43,390 --> 00:04:45,933
But then usual
tricks do help across

99
00:04:45,933 --> 00:04:47,350
the neural net
literature which is

100
00:04:47,350 --> 00:04:50,585
like having
multi-head attentions,

101
00:04:50,585 --> 00:04:52,210
having skip connection
and layer norms.

102
00:04:52,210 --> 00:04:55,390
So all of these things,
they are not only

103
00:04:55,390 --> 00:04:57,820
getting gains for
transformers themselves,

104
00:04:57,820 --> 00:05:01,510
but they can be just applied to
any single other architecture

105
00:05:01,510 --> 00:05:03,490
also.

106
00:05:03,490 --> 00:05:05,980
The other thing which
is helping this research

107
00:05:05,980 --> 00:05:09,370
is basically the compute power
is getting better and better.

108
00:05:09,370 --> 00:05:12,670
So all of these big
companies are just

109
00:05:12,670 --> 00:05:14,890
throwing massive amounts
of computing resources

110
00:05:14,890 --> 00:05:18,880
at solving very, very
simple and trivial tasks.

111
00:05:18,880 --> 00:05:21,170
The top of the hill being
the switch transformer

112
00:05:21,170 --> 00:05:22,795
which was discussed
in the course also.

113
00:05:26,230 --> 00:05:29,290
But one of the thing which I
think started all of this trend

114
00:05:29,290 --> 00:05:31,240
was ELMo, which
was just learning

115
00:05:31,240 --> 00:05:33,190
these contextualized
representations

116
00:05:33,190 --> 00:05:34,840
for natural language processing.

117
00:05:34,840 --> 00:05:37,540
And that model right
here was perhaps one

118
00:05:37,540 --> 00:05:45,640
of the first kind of
model 1.0 or something

119
00:05:45,640 --> 00:05:48,310
or 1.1 in terms of
bringing and ushering

120
00:05:48,310 --> 00:05:50,740
in the whole revolution.

121
00:05:50,740 --> 00:05:54,550
You can see how similar these
kinds of models look like.

122
00:05:54,550 --> 00:05:57,250
BERT was basically
like inspired heavily

123
00:05:57,250 --> 00:06:01,390
from ELMo in which they just
replaced one of the LSTM layers

124
00:06:01,390 --> 00:06:04,300
with transformer models.

125
00:06:04,300 --> 00:06:08,480
So a point to note
also is irrespective

126
00:06:08,480 --> 00:06:10,810
of natural language
processing or other domain,

127
00:06:10,810 --> 00:06:13,060
these can be adopted in
a variety of domains.

128
00:06:13,060 --> 00:06:17,800
And for today's talk, I'll be
just adopting them to audio.

129
00:06:17,800 --> 00:06:20,830
So I basically start
with introducing

130
00:06:20,830 --> 00:06:22,720
people what audio
representations are

131
00:06:22,720 --> 00:06:25,910
and just for the
sake of completeness

132
00:06:25,910 --> 00:06:28,310
talk about spectrograms.

133
00:06:28,310 --> 00:06:31,420
So you can take any
time domain signal

134
00:06:31,420 --> 00:06:34,630
and you can
decompose that signal

135
00:06:34,630 --> 00:06:39,770
into a variety of
business functions.

136
00:06:39,770 --> 00:06:41,740
And if you take up
a Fourier transform,

137
00:06:41,740 --> 00:06:46,090
you're kind of like decomposing
the actual time domain

138
00:06:46,090 --> 00:06:49,340
signal into its sinusoidal
pieces components.

139
00:06:49,340 --> 00:06:52,460
So if you have a
waveform here like this,

140
00:06:52,460 --> 00:06:55,270
which is a sum of
three pure sinusoids,

141
00:06:55,270 --> 00:06:57,490
then their sum
basically is this.

142
00:06:57,490 --> 00:06:59,740
And you can see that
when you take a Fourier

143
00:06:59,740 --> 00:07:03,040
transform and its
magnitude, you kind of

144
00:07:03,040 --> 00:07:08,140
have their strength of the
individual components shown

145
00:07:08,140 --> 00:07:08,870
here.

146
00:07:08,870 --> 00:07:11,290
So you can take
another waveform, let's

147
00:07:11,290 --> 00:07:16,240
say a square wave, and what you
have is basically much richer

148
00:07:16,240 --> 00:07:18,250
sinusoidal decomposition
because it is

149
00:07:18,250 --> 00:07:19,810
kind of a discontinuous signal.

150
00:07:19,810 --> 00:07:22,270
So you need many more
sinusoids to represent

151
00:07:22,270 --> 00:07:25,360
that particular signal as
close to the actual signal

152
00:07:25,360 --> 00:07:27,010
as possible.

153
00:07:27,010 --> 00:07:28,360
And here also you can see that--

154
00:07:28,360 --> 00:07:30,130
OK, if this was a
square wave, then

155
00:07:30,130 --> 00:07:35,980
it is actually made up
of a lot of sinusoids

156
00:07:35,980 --> 00:07:39,820
where each of the bar here
represents the strength

157
00:07:39,820 --> 00:07:42,310
of the particular sinusoid.

158
00:07:42,310 --> 00:07:44,770
From an optimization
perspective, I mean,

159
00:07:44,770 --> 00:07:47,110
this right away is
suboptimal, right,

160
00:07:47,110 --> 00:07:51,130
because you're kind of fixing
up the number of sinusoids

161
00:07:51,130 --> 00:07:53,320
you are using for
representing a square wave.

162
00:07:53,320 --> 00:07:56,150
I would have rather
used a basis function

163
00:07:56,150 --> 00:08:00,740
which was a square wave itself
than a sinusoidal signal,

164
00:08:00,740 --> 00:08:01,360
right?

165
00:08:01,360 --> 00:08:02,890
The second thing
is like even if you

166
00:08:02,890 --> 00:08:08,080
are taking a sinusoid signal,
we kind of are just putting them

167
00:08:08,080 --> 00:08:09,520
in a equidistant space.

168
00:08:09,520 --> 00:08:11,800
So you are kind of dividing
the whole frequency

169
00:08:11,800 --> 00:08:14,500
axis into equidistant bends.

170
00:08:14,500 --> 00:08:16,090
And each of the
bends are responsible

171
00:08:16,090 --> 00:08:19,690
for like a particular
sinusoid a lot.

172
00:08:19,690 --> 00:08:22,390
So that is like a traditional
Fourier representation

173
00:08:22,390 --> 00:08:24,160
for representing any signal.

174
00:08:28,930 --> 00:08:30,550
What are spectrograms?

175
00:08:30,550 --> 00:08:34,960
But in reality, all of these
signals are discontinuous.

176
00:08:34,960 --> 00:08:37,299
All of these signals
vary quite a bit, right?

177
00:08:37,299 --> 00:08:40,059
So you can have a
signal while I'm

178
00:08:40,059 --> 00:08:42,760
speaking which is like a square
wave for a certain period

179
00:08:42,760 --> 00:08:44,770
of time and then
it gets sinusoidal

180
00:08:44,770 --> 00:08:46,640
and then it becomes
something else.

181
00:08:46,640 --> 00:08:48,970
So what we really
need is in a way

182
00:08:48,970 --> 00:08:52,840
to kind of take batches
of the input signal

183
00:08:52,840 --> 00:08:56,363
and take Fourier transform
of these individual batches.

184
00:08:56,363 --> 00:08:58,030
I'm deliberately using
the word batches,

185
00:08:58,030 --> 00:08:59,697
but you can like--
in traditional terms,

186
00:08:59,697 --> 00:09:02,270
you're windowing the signal.

187
00:09:02,270 --> 00:09:05,140
So right here, you can see that
you have a continuous signal,

188
00:09:05,140 --> 00:09:09,130
you keep on windowing it, you
apply the Fourier transform,

189
00:09:09,130 --> 00:09:12,130
and what you get is
basically like a spectrogram

190
00:09:12,130 --> 00:09:14,200
representation of the signal.

191
00:09:14,200 --> 00:09:16,420
So right here, what
you're seeing basically

192
00:09:16,420 --> 00:09:19,690
is with each of the slices,
the signal kind of looked

193
00:09:19,690 --> 00:09:22,090
like this after taking
the Fourier transform

194
00:09:22,090 --> 00:09:24,880
with the waveform,
which is there below.

195
00:09:24,880 --> 00:09:26,990
And what you do is for
spectrogram representation,

196
00:09:26,990 --> 00:09:29,590
you keep on stacking these
Fourier transform slides,

197
00:09:29,590 --> 00:09:31,780
the magnitude of the
Fourier transform slices.

198
00:09:31,780 --> 00:09:34,810
And in this way, you kind of
get like a 2D representation

199
00:09:34,810 --> 00:09:36,763
of audio segments.

200
00:09:36,763 --> 00:09:38,680
And if you're coming
from a vision background,

201
00:09:38,680 --> 00:09:40,327
it is basically all
of the things which

202
00:09:40,327 --> 00:09:42,160
you are doing in vision
would just work well

203
00:09:42,160 --> 00:09:47,350
if you just apply them to these
2D spectral representations.

204
00:09:47,350 --> 00:09:49,840
I'll quickly play
how the spectrograms

205
00:09:49,840 --> 00:09:53,470
look like for a wide area
of like common sounds.

206
00:10:35,570 --> 00:10:38,390
So you could see like
for spectrograms you

207
00:10:38,390 --> 00:10:41,070
have kind of like a time
axis on your x-axis.

208
00:10:41,070 --> 00:10:43,670
And then, you have a
frequency axis on y-axis.

209
00:10:43,670 --> 00:10:46,190
And then, for whatever is
your signal of interest,

210
00:10:46,190 --> 00:10:48,890
you're basically putting
these slices together,

211
00:10:48,890 --> 00:10:51,020
and different sound gives
you different spectral

212
00:10:51,020 --> 00:10:51,710
representation.

213
00:10:51,710 --> 00:10:54,660
So it's kind of a vision
problem just in this sort

214
00:10:54,660 --> 00:10:55,610
of like Fourier space.

215
00:10:58,390 --> 00:11:01,220
So there can be different
kinds of representations also.

216
00:11:01,220 --> 00:11:06,350
So one, you could just take
these slices of Fourier

217
00:11:06,350 --> 00:11:09,350
transform and then do like
a linear mapping to them

218
00:11:09,350 --> 00:11:13,070
so that you are kind of
in a way making these

219
00:11:13,070 --> 00:11:14,690
as close to how humans hear.

220
00:11:14,690 --> 00:11:17,360
So you can have a
log of the frequency

221
00:11:17,360 --> 00:11:19,250
and the y-axis and sort
of common frequency.

222
00:11:19,250 --> 00:11:22,100
And then, you get a constant
Q-like representation.

223
00:11:22,100 --> 00:11:23,600
The advantage of
this being you can

224
00:11:23,600 --> 00:11:25,800
see that for
different frequencies,

225
00:11:25,800 --> 00:11:27,710
the spacing in
between the harmonics

226
00:11:27,710 --> 00:11:28,890
kind of remains the same.

227
00:11:28,890 --> 00:11:31,040
So if you are creating
unusual filters,

228
00:11:31,040 --> 00:11:33,890
then that's of a huge
advantage because the signal--

229
00:11:33,890 --> 00:11:35,638
one component of the
invariance is gone,

230
00:11:35,638 --> 00:11:37,430
and you can just learn
these filters, which

231
00:11:37,430 --> 00:11:41,870
are catching on to these
constant templates of Fourier

232
00:11:41,870 --> 00:11:43,160
slices.

233
00:11:43,160 --> 00:11:45,160
You can have Mel
filterbank coefficients,

234
00:11:45,160 --> 00:11:48,980
or you can have the
raw waveform also.

235
00:11:48,980 --> 00:11:50,540
For raw waveforms,
basically there

236
00:11:50,540 --> 00:11:53,308
are two things which we
have to keep in mind.

237
00:11:53,308 --> 00:11:54,350
One is the sampling rate.

238
00:11:54,350 --> 00:11:57,230
So we kind of take
the continuous signal,

239
00:11:57,230 --> 00:12:01,100
and then we discretize
the continuous signal.

240
00:12:01,100 --> 00:12:03,410
So one parameter is how
fast we are sampling

241
00:12:03,410 --> 00:12:05,220
the continuous signal,
so that's typically

242
00:12:05,220 --> 00:12:08,510
on the order of like 16,000
or 8,000 times a second

243
00:12:08,510 --> 00:12:10,430
if you're on telephonic speech.

244
00:12:10,430 --> 00:12:13,760
The other thing which we
also have is how many levels

245
00:12:13,760 --> 00:12:15,480
we are dividing
your vertical axis.

246
00:12:15,480 --> 00:12:17,810
So in this case, you can
see that each of the dots

247
00:12:17,810 --> 00:12:19,500
is basically one level.

248
00:12:19,500 --> 00:12:21,700
And typically, people
use 8-bit quantizers

249
00:12:21,700 --> 00:12:23,210
or 16-bit quantizers.

250
00:12:23,210 --> 00:12:25,430
So in a way, you can think
about that for every one

251
00:12:25,430 --> 00:12:27,560
second of audio
which we would hear,

252
00:12:27,560 --> 00:12:31,010
you would have 16,000 samples.

253
00:12:31,010 --> 00:12:32,750
And then each of
the 16,000 samples

254
00:12:32,750 --> 00:12:38,600
are allowed to take one of
the levels between 0 to 255.

255
00:12:38,600 --> 00:12:41,930
If I can take the problem of
like continuous audio and just

256
00:12:41,930 --> 00:12:45,230
have it in terms of this
sort of discrete space,

257
00:12:45,230 --> 00:12:47,570
then basically I'm just
going to the territory

258
00:12:47,570 --> 00:12:51,170
of doing language modeling.

259
00:12:51,170 --> 00:12:53,360
In my first paper,
so I discussed this,

260
00:12:53,360 --> 00:12:56,270
how can we do
generative modeling

261
00:12:56,270 --> 00:13:01,520
for raw audio, which is similar
to wavenets using transformers.

262
00:13:01,520 --> 00:13:03,140
I'll be putting QR codes.

263
00:13:03,140 --> 00:13:05,540
If you like the
stuff what I'm doing,

264
00:13:05,540 --> 00:13:07,910
and if you think that
this is relevant to you,

265
00:13:07,910 --> 00:13:13,040
please cite or please have a
look in terms of the QR codes,

266
00:13:13,040 --> 00:13:13,670
so, yeah.

267
00:13:13,670 --> 00:13:17,270
So I'll start with the first
subtopic of today's talk,

268
00:13:17,270 --> 00:13:23,660
which is what are
wavenets and how

269
00:13:23,660 --> 00:13:25,910
do we do like this generative
modeling with raw audio?

270
00:13:26,850 --> 00:13:28,880
So in single word, you
can think about this

271
00:13:28,880 --> 00:13:33,180
as doing like language modeling,
or these 255 states of audio.

272
00:13:33,180 --> 00:13:35,850
So you can throw on your
favorite transformer

273
00:13:35,850 --> 00:13:41,570
like Transformer-XL or GPT or
whatever you want to call it.

274
00:13:41,570 --> 00:13:43,400
And just treat the
problem as if you're

275
00:13:43,400 --> 00:13:45,900
trying to predict what are
the levels that are 255.

276
00:13:45,900 --> 00:13:49,520
And you have to predict the next
level given a certain context.

277
00:13:49,520 --> 00:13:50,910
That's what Wavenet was doing.

278
00:13:50,910 --> 00:13:55,370
So the way you are modeling
the probability distribution

279
00:13:55,370 --> 00:13:57,590
of continuous space
is basically you're

280
00:13:57,590 --> 00:14:00,350
trying to predict what's the
probability of the next sample

281
00:14:00,350 --> 00:14:02,600
given some sparse context.

282
00:14:02,600 --> 00:14:04,760
And Wavenet has
been hugely popular

283
00:14:04,760 --> 00:14:07,130
because it has over
3,000 citations.

284
00:14:07,130 --> 00:14:11,120
And it has been a core building
block for almost all speech

285
00:14:11,120 --> 00:14:12,980
and audio related problems.

286
00:14:12,980 --> 00:14:15,110
You can think about
speech to text or text

287
00:14:15,110 --> 00:14:19,070
to speech synthesis, instrument
conversion, packet loss

288
00:14:19,070 --> 00:14:21,720
concealment of internet
speech denoising.

289
00:14:21,720 --> 00:14:25,550
So wherever there is some sort
of element of modifying audio,

290
00:14:25,550 --> 00:14:30,500
people have been using Wavenet
as a core building block.

291
00:14:30,500 --> 00:14:32,930
And raw waveform synthesis
has been difficult

292
00:14:32,930 --> 00:14:36,800
because just the magnitude
of the problem, if I'm just

293
00:14:36,800 --> 00:14:39,050
trying to synthesize
10 seconds of audio

294
00:14:39,050 --> 00:14:41,750
would just amount to like
me having a probability

295
00:14:41,750 --> 00:14:45,800
distribution over
160,000 samples.

296
00:14:45,800 --> 00:14:49,160
And I could tell this stuff
because our ears are very, very

297
00:14:49,160 --> 00:14:51,050
sensitive to subtle changes.

298
00:14:51,050 --> 00:14:54,230
If I'm off by one
pixel in an image--

299
00:14:54,230 --> 00:14:56,510
the image, like
my eyes would not

300
00:14:56,510 --> 00:15:01,430
be as susceptible to noticing
that effect versus if I'm

301
00:15:01,430 --> 00:15:04,940
off by, say, a few
samples in an audio,

302
00:15:04,940 --> 00:15:08,630
it will just catch our
ears pretty quickly.

303
00:15:08,630 --> 00:15:11,000
People have been trying
raw audio synthesis a lot

304
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:12,410
in the past.

305
00:15:12,410 --> 00:15:15,770
And before all of the
internet and transformer based

306
00:15:15,770 --> 00:15:20,330
approaches kind of like
WaveRNNs and Sample RNNs

307
00:15:20,330 --> 00:15:25,850
were kind of like state
of the art models.

308
00:15:25,850 --> 00:15:28,460
On the right I've shown
a Sample RNN model

309
00:15:28,460 --> 00:15:32,852
which kind of like models
the probability distribution

310
00:15:32,852 --> 00:15:34,310
of what's going to
come next, given

311
00:15:34,310 --> 00:15:36,640
the past at multiple levels.

312
00:15:36,640 --> 00:15:40,250
And this was well done
by Yoshua Bengio at Mila.

313
00:15:40,250 --> 00:15:42,145
But you can closely see.

314
00:15:42,145 --> 00:15:43,520
If you just see
this architecture

315
00:15:43,520 --> 00:15:46,310
versus a transformer
architecture, in a way,

316
00:15:46,310 --> 00:15:48,710
these are starting to
get very, very similar,

317
00:15:48,710 --> 00:15:51,590
because what you're trying to
do is that for the probability

318
00:15:51,590 --> 00:15:53,780
distribution here,
you're trying to see

319
00:15:53,780 --> 00:15:55,939
a lot of local substructures.

320
00:15:55,939 --> 00:15:58,022
And then you keep on doing
it over and over again.

321
00:15:58,022 --> 00:16:00,910
And you can draw parallels,
like, OK, attention mechanism

322
00:16:00,910 --> 00:16:04,012
should also kind of be
doing the same thing.

323
00:16:04,012 --> 00:16:09,220
So this was the kind of
the literature in the past.

324
00:16:09,220 --> 00:16:13,120
What we tried to do was we
just had the WaveNet model

325
00:16:13,120 --> 00:16:15,790
and we tried to see whether
transformers can beat them.

326
00:16:15,790 --> 00:16:18,160
And our intuition was it
should be able to beat them,

327
00:16:18,160 --> 00:16:23,020
because they are successful
all over the other domains,

328
00:16:23,020 --> 00:16:24,230
like in language modeling.

329
00:16:24,230 --> 00:16:28,720
So it should-- it should do
that for raw waveforms also.

330
00:16:28,720 --> 00:16:31,840
We also try to see whether
we can circumvent the order n

331
00:16:31,840 --> 00:16:37,090
constraint by conditioning
on the context itself.

332
00:16:37,090 --> 00:16:40,392
And we did not go for
specific applications.

333
00:16:40,392 --> 00:16:42,850
And we just said, OK, it just
looks like modeling behavior.

334
00:16:42,850 --> 00:16:45,650
How will they do?

335
00:16:45,650 --> 00:16:48,280
So the data set for this was
just like real-world piano

336
00:16:48,280 --> 00:16:49,300
recordings.

337
00:16:49,300 --> 00:16:53,410
So actual sound
should not matter,

338
00:16:53,410 --> 00:16:57,820
because the model is agnostic
to what it is being thrown in.

339
00:16:57,820 --> 00:16:59,680
And the setup was
exactly the same.

340
00:16:59,680 --> 00:17:01,720
You are given a certain
context and have

341
00:17:01,720 --> 00:17:03,610
to predict the next sample.

342
00:17:03,610 --> 00:17:05,500
You do the same
thing with WaveNet.

343
00:17:05,500 --> 00:17:09,940
You do the exact same thing
with transformer-based GPT

344
00:17:09,940 --> 00:17:14,130
kind of a model and
see how well they do.

345
00:17:14,130 --> 00:17:18,010
I'll briefly chat about
what WaveNet models are.

346
00:17:18,010 --> 00:17:20,560
So WaveNet was kind
of like a convolution

347
00:17:20,560 --> 00:17:23,230
based model which
was getting rid

348
00:17:23,230 --> 00:17:25,329
of like all of the
vanishing gradient problem

349
00:17:25,329 --> 00:17:29,590
by just creating a sequential
problem as being learned

350
00:17:29,590 --> 00:17:31,450
by a convolutional model.

351
00:17:31,450 --> 00:17:33,550
So what they did
was basically have

352
00:17:33,550 --> 00:17:38,290
these sort of dilation layers
or a convolution with dilations,

353
00:17:38,290 --> 00:17:41,980
which is basically I kind of
skip in every subsequent layer

354
00:17:41,980 --> 00:17:43,190
by one sample.

355
00:17:43,190 --> 00:17:47,050
So you can see if I have
a dilation factor of 2

356
00:17:47,050 --> 00:17:50,380
with a kernel size of 2, I would
get this kind of a topology,

357
00:17:50,380 --> 00:17:52,690
where my convolution filters
in the very first layer

358
00:17:52,690 --> 00:17:55,090
are just combining
the first two samples.

359
00:17:55,090 --> 00:17:57,620
Then I skip by one
in the next layer.

360
00:17:57,620 --> 00:17:59,530
And then I skip
by three, which is

361
00:17:59,530 --> 00:18:03,310
I look at like the fourth one
in the next layer, and so on.

362
00:18:03,310 --> 00:18:04,580
The loss is still the same.

363
00:18:04,580 --> 00:18:06,580
So I have this network.

364
00:18:06,580 --> 00:18:08,020
I learn a latent space.

365
00:18:08,020 --> 00:18:10,930
And then I have a cross in--
categorical cross entropy

366
00:18:10,930 --> 00:18:13,750
loss, which is basically I
have to predict the next sample

367
00:18:13,750 --> 00:18:16,720
given the previous one.

368
00:18:16,720 --> 00:18:20,800
And I just do the exact same
thing with transformers, also.

369
00:18:20,800 --> 00:18:23,140
But then I have to
make sure that I do it

370
00:18:23,140 --> 00:18:24,110
in a causal manner.

371
00:18:24,110 --> 00:18:25,780
So I have something
which is very

372
00:18:25,780 --> 00:18:29,350
similar to GPT, in which I have
causal masks in my attention

373
00:18:29,350 --> 00:18:30,310
mechanism.

374
00:18:30,310 --> 00:18:32,280
And I keep doing it
over and over again.

375
00:18:32,280 --> 00:18:35,560
So you have self-attention.

376
00:18:35,560 --> 00:18:37,390
After that, you have
feed forward layers.

377
00:18:37,390 --> 00:18:40,420
You just have a stack of
these transformer blocks

378
00:18:40,420 --> 00:18:43,360
and see how would they do.

379
00:18:43,360 --> 00:18:47,710
So I said intuitively
it should work.

380
00:18:47,710 --> 00:18:52,390
It should be doing better than
our base WaveNet models, right?

381
00:18:52,390 --> 00:18:55,190
Because if you look
at the topology,

382
00:18:55,190 --> 00:18:57,440
we are kind of defining a
topology on our ow, right?

383
00:18:57,440 --> 00:19:02,140
So what if the current
prediction at say, layer one

384
00:19:02,140 --> 00:19:07,967
were to depend on very way
back samples, say, instead

385
00:19:07,967 --> 00:19:09,550
of the second sample,
the 10th sample.

386
00:19:09,550 --> 00:19:12,720
So we are kind of ignoring
all of that topology which

387
00:19:12,720 --> 00:19:14,470
would have been important
for a prediction

388
00:19:14,470 --> 00:19:16,090
of this particular task.

389
00:19:16,090 --> 00:19:19,660
Whereas, our transformers with
the self-attention mechanism

390
00:19:19,660 --> 00:19:22,960
can just learn, OK, which part
of the samples are important

391
00:19:22,960 --> 00:19:24,020
and which are not.

392
00:19:24,020 --> 00:19:26,740
And you can keep on
doing it iteratively.

393
00:19:26,740 --> 00:19:30,910
So it made sense to us
that, OK, transform layer

394
00:19:30,910 --> 00:19:34,990
should be doing way better
than WaveNet models.

395
00:19:34,990 --> 00:19:37,720
The second thing which we
came across was like, OK,

396
00:19:37,720 --> 00:19:40,690
we cannot have a lot of context.

397
00:19:40,690 --> 00:19:42,490
For example, the
attention mechanism

398
00:19:42,490 --> 00:19:46,470
needs to store all of
those of order n squares.

399
00:19:46,470 --> 00:19:50,290
In this case, if I'm storing
data at 100 milliseconds,

400
00:19:50,290 --> 00:19:52,630
then I have about 1,600 samples.

401
00:19:52,630 --> 00:19:56,890
And I need to store 1,600
by 1,600 at multiple layers.

402
00:19:56,890 --> 00:20:00,670
And it just becomes
a huge problem

403
00:20:00,670 --> 00:20:03,190
with the data, problem
with the memory constraint.

404
00:20:03,190 --> 00:20:09,010
So what we said was, OK, what if
we just use the context itself

405
00:20:09,010 --> 00:20:10,900
as a latent code?

406
00:20:10,900 --> 00:20:16,510
So in order to have much better
representation at every layer,

407
00:20:16,510 --> 00:20:19,970
we cannot have the huge,
big attention matrices.

408
00:20:19,970 --> 00:20:22,000
So what we said
was we would just

409
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:25,930
do sample-wise conditioning,
and through our CNN layers

410
00:20:25,930 --> 00:20:28,600
just to understand what
the latent code would be.

411
00:20:28,600 --> 00:20:31,510
So you still have an
attention mechanism or just

412
00:20:31,510 --> 00:20:32,980
a past context.

413
00:20:32,980 --> 00:20:36,340
But then I'm also
conditioning at every sample,

414
00:20:36,340 --> 00:20:40,480
OK, what the next sample should
be given on-the-spot context

415
00:20:40,480 --> 00:20:41,230
embedding.

416
00:20:41,230 --> 00:20:42,790
And if you think
about it, in a way,

417
00:20:42,790 --> 00:20:45,610
it is like, OK, if there are
five or six notes being played

418
00:20:45,610 --> 00:20:47,860
in a piano, then
I'm kind of certain

419
00:20:47,860 --> 00:20:50,120
which notes will be
played to a certain extent

420
00:20:50,120 --> 00:20:52,760
if I just throw in a CNN layer.

421
00:20:52,760 --> 00:20:55,360
So I'll use that
information along with what

422
00:20:55,360 --> 00:20:57,490
my transformers are learning.

423
00:20:57,490 --> 00:20:59,050
And then I would condition it.

424
00:20:59,050 --> 00:21:03,210
And I would just use that
to predict the next sample.

425
00:21:03,210 --> 00:21:04,830
So for the evaluation
criteria, we

426
00:21:04,830 --> 00:21:08,790
did not look for negative
log likelihood scores.

427
00:21:08,790 --> 00:21:12,690
We just looked at how well
our prediction task was.

428
00:21:12,690 --> 00:21:15,960
So we took a stacked
WaveNet, which

429
00:21:15,960 --> 00:21:18,690
was implemented by
DeepMind and saw that, OK,

430
00:21:18,690 --> 00:21:22,230
what was the performance
using their benchmarks,

431
00:21:22,230 --> 00:21:25,890
and even bigger
stacked WaveNets.

432
00:21:25,890 --> 00:21:27,570
We then took--
started to increase

433
00:21:27,570 --> 00:21:29,610
the complexity of
transformers and started

434
00:21:29,610 --> 00:21:33,660
to see whatever we had proposed
in terms of conditioning

435
00:21:33,660 --> 00:21:37,440
on the vanilla
transformer architectures

436
00:21:37,440 --> 00:21:39,510
to see how well they do.

437
00:21:39,510 --> 00:21:43,630
We did not look for an
application specific problem,

438
00:21:43,630 --> 00:21:45,510
which is basically like--

439
00:21:45,510 --> 00:21:48,810
we don't look at how well
perceptual tasks are for,

440
00:21:48,810 --> 00:21:51,570
like, say, text-to-speech
synthesis or speech denoising.

441
00:21:51,570 --> 00:21:53,250
We just look at,
OK, if we are trying

442
00:21:53,250 --> 00:21:55,860
to model this using
a cross entropy loss,

443
00:21:55,860 --> 00:21:58,590
then with the same
model with the same loss

444
00:21:58,590 --> 00:22:03,480
function, how well they do on
similar kind of parameters?

445
00:22:03,480 --> 00:22:06,150
So this was the first
kind of sub-block

446
00:22:06,150 --> 00:22:09,180
of how can we use transformers
for generative modeling.

447
00:22:12,180 --> 00:22:15,390
For the second problem,
I'll do a quick headaway

448
00:22:15,390 --> 00:22:19,920
on how can we use
transformers for doing

449
00:22:19,920 --> 00:22:22,290
language modeling, which
is kind of becoming

450
00:22:22,290 --> 00:22:24,870
a really fancy term right now.

451
00:22:24,870 --> 00:22:29,010
And this work was done by
Julius Smith way back in 2020.

452
00:22:29,010 --> 00:22:32,580
And the goal of this was
can we kind of, in a way,

453
00:22:32,580 --> 00:22:37,440
do language modeling with
continuous audio sequences.

454
00:22:37,440 --> 00:22:41,250
And I briefly mentioned
about that in this part of--

455
00:22:41,250 --> 00:22:42,990
in this sub-block of the talk.

456
00:22:45,750 --> 00:22:48,510
And this is in regard for
solving acoustic scene

457
00:22:48,510 --> 00:22:49,110
understanding.

458
00:22:49,110 --> 00:22:54,120
Which is basically if I'm
given a chunk of audio,

459
00:22:54,120 --> 00:22:57,130
then I want to understand
what's in there.

460
00:22:57,130 --> 00:23:01,890
And if we could do that
well, then, in a way,

461
00:23:01,890 --> 00:23:06,040
we can do a lot of
fancy, nice applications.

462
00:23:06,040 --> 00:23:09,060
So for example, if you think
about self-driving cars.

463
00:23:09,060 --> 00:23:12,210
So Waymo has started to
incorporate microphones

464
00:23:12,210 --> 00:23:13,480
into their self-driving cars.

465
00:23:13,480 --> 00:23:13,980
Why?

466
00:23:13,980 --> 00:23:16,050
Because say, if there
is an ambulance coming,

467
00:23:16,050 --> 00:23:18,930
or if there is a
fire truck coming,

468
00:23:18,930 --> 00:23:23,040
then that sound would be
picked up way, way before even

469
00:23:23,040 --> 00:23:25,680
than lidars or
even their sensors.

470
00:23:25,680 --> 00:23:28,920
So we want to understand
that and take actions

471
00:23:28,920 --> 00:23:30,880
based upon that.

472
00:23:30,880 --> 00:23:33,330
Apple, during COVID, did
a handwashing detection

473
00:23:33,330 --> 00:23:34,470
on their Apple Watch.

474
00:23:34,470 --> 00:23:37,860
Because if you could detect when
someone is washing their hands,

475
00:23:37,860 --> 00:23:40,500
then you can, in
a way, tell people

476
00:23:40,500 --> 00:23:42,750
that you need to wash
hands for 20 seconds.

477
00:23:42,750 --> 00:23:46,980
And then that can be built
upon as a cool application.

478
00:23:46,980 --> 00:23:49,550
It can be used for
music recommendations.

479
00:23:49,550 --> 00:23:51,390
So Spotify, YouTube
Music, kind of

480
00:23:51,390 --> 00:23:53,760
gives very, very
good songs which

481
00:23:53,760 --> 00:23:57,090
you are listening to which
are similar in content

482
00:23:57,090 --> 00:23:59,340
that you would perhaps like.

483
00:23:59,340 --> 00:24:02,160
It can also give really
cool applications, say,

484
00:24:02,160 --> 00:24:05,850
people have tried detecting
depression from audio.

485
00:24:05,850 --> 00:24:08,740
Or I could detect whether
I'm coughing or not,

486
00:24:08,740 --> 00:24:10,440
or I'm sneezing
or not, and these

487
00:24:10,440 --> 00:24:15,120
can be good medical device--
medical applications which

488
00:24:15,120 --> 00:24:18,210
can be used along with the
current diagnosis what doctor

489
00:24:18,210 --> 00:24:20,550
provides.

490
00:24:20,550 --> 00:24:23,340
So the question was
basically, for us,

491
00:24:23,340 --> 00:24:26,640
was how can we do
language modeling

492
00:24:26,640 --> 00:24:29,040
in a continuous audio domain.

493
00:24:29,040 --> 00:24:31,290
And secondly, how
can we train models?

494
00:24:31,290 --> 00:24:34,860
Or how should we
approach doing this?

495
00:24:34,860 --> 00:24:39,180
So this kind of recipe has
become very, very popular

496
00:24:39,180 --> 00:24:42,210
these days in terms of how
would you approach this problem.

497
00:24:42,210 --> 00:24:46,350
It started with openAI
and, to a certain extent,

498
00:24:46,350 --> 00:24:51,000
DeepMind proposing that
in terms of VQ-VAE models.

499
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:53,730
But it turns out
transformers love operating

500
00:24:53,730 --> 00:24:56,550
in discrete spaces as of now.

501
00:24:56,550 --> 00:25:01,260
And what they kind of do is as
long as your representations

502
00:25:01,260 --> 00:25:02,850
are discrete, they
are very, very good

503
00:25:02,850 --> 00:25:06,490
at modeling what's
going to come next.

504
00:25:06,490 --> 00:25:08,460
So what people
have been proposing

505
00:25:08,460 --> 00:25:15,690
as a workaround is you could
take up your favorite embedding

506
00:25:15,690 --> 00:25:16,470
in some manner.

507
00:25:16,470 --> 00:25:19,980
You could take VQ-VAE embeddings
or you could take a wav2vec.

508
00:25:19,980 --> 00:25:26,760
Or in terms of video, you can
just do classic VGG or ResNet

509
00:25:26,760 --> 00:25:27,870
embeddings.

510
00:25:27,870 --> 00:25:31,080
You can apply K means
clustering to it.

511
00:25:31,080 --> 00:25:34,290
And K means clustering would
give you the discrete codes.

512
00:25:34,290 --> 00:25:37,200
You do language modeling
on those discrete codes.

513
00:25:37,200 --> 00:25:39,300
And you predict the next code.

514
00:25:39,300 --> 00:25:41,560
And in a way, if
you're doing this,

515
00:25:41,560 --> 00:25:45,092
then you're kind of doing
language modeling for audio.

516
00:25:45,092 --> 00:25:46,800
And if you need to
get back to the audio,

517
00:25:46,800 --> 00:25:49,050
then you already
saw with WaveNet

518
00:25:49,050 --> 00:25:51,780
that you can condition
the WaveNet model to give

519
00:25:51,780 --> 00:25:53,530
continuous output.

520
00:25:53,530 --> 00:25:55,350
So you can use those
codes to get back

521
00:25:55,350 --> 00:26:00,270
to the audio similar to
what jukebox and openAI did.

522
00:26:00,270 --> 00:26:06,000
So I'll quickly mention about
what vector quantization is.

523
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:08,700
It's one of the most
underutilized algorithms,

524
00:26:08,700 --> 00:26:09,870
to be honest.

525
00:26:09,870 --> 00:26:12,240
And what it does
is basically gives,

526
00:26:12,240 --> 00:26:16,950
in a way, discrete codes to
continuous embedding spaces.

527
00:26:16,950 --> 00:26:18,000
So how does it do it?

528
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:23,580
So you basically have
an embedding space,

529
00:26:23,580 --> 00:26:25,230
let's say, in 2D right here.

530
00:26:25,230 --> 00:26:27,090
You define what are
the number of clusters

531
00:26:27,090 --> 00:26:29,250
you want to put each of them in.

532
00:26:29,250 --> 00:26:30,570
You run k means.

533
00:26:30,570 --> 00:26:33,900
And you would certainly
get these patches

534
00:26:33,900 --> 00:26:36,180
of where all of
these embeddings,

535
00:26:36,180 --> 00:26:38,280
or what would be the
representative embedding

536
00:26:38,280 --> 00:26:40,710
of a continuous embedding.

537
00:26:40,710 --> 00:26:42,220
You can take all
of those patches.

538
00:26:42,220 --> 00:26:44,012
And you can see-- you
can just number them,

539
00:26:44,012 --> 00:26:45,850
or you can just like list them.

540
00:26:45,850 --> 00:26:49,890
So in this case, you can perhaps
have 25 numbers or 20 numbers,

541
00:26:49,890 --> 00:26:53,520
which are, in a way, mapping
from a continuous embedding

542
00:26:53,520 --> 00:26:57,070
to a discrete token.

543
00:26:57,070 --> 00:26:58,550
This is another
example right here.

544
00:26:58,550 --> 00:27:02,380
So in our case,
what we did was we

545
00:27:02,380 --> 00:27:08,050
took patches of spectrogram,
which are basically very small

546
00:27:08,050 --> 00:27:10,810
patches across time
and then shared all

547
00:27:10,810 --> 00:27:13,360
across the frequency axis.

548
00:27:13,360 --> 00:27:16,630
You take those patches, you
learn embedding representation.

549
00:27:16,630 --> 00:27:19,630
In our case, it was just
three-layer autoencoder

550
00:27:19,630 --> 00:27:22,930
fully connected encoders
with three layers of decoders

551
00:27:22,930 --> 00:27:25,420
and have a bottleneck
layer in between.

552
00:27:25,420 --> 00:27:27,040
So that bottleneck
layer, basically,

553
00:27:27,040 --> 00:27:29,320
is kind of similar to
this kind of diagram

554
00:27:29,320 --> 00:27:33,520
in 64 dimensional space
or 128 dimensional space.

555
00:27:33,520 --> 00:27:35,380
You take up those
bottleneck codes.

556
00:27:35,380 --> 00:27:37,790
And then you run K
means clustering on it.

557
00:27:37,790 --> 00:27:43,540
Suddenly, you-- in a way,
you can find discrete codes

558
00:27:43,540 --> 00:27:48,070
for continuous embedding spaces,
or even continuous signals.

559
00:27:48,070 --> 00:27:49,870
And since we know
that transformers kind

560
00:27:49,870 --> 00:27:52,390
of love operating
in discrete spaces,

561
00:27:52,390 --> 00:27:55,210
you can just apply
language modeling now.

562
00:27:55,210 --> 00:27:58,430
And then you can
see what you can do.

563
00:27:58,430 --> 00:28:02,260
So in our case, we just had
very simple, three-layer

564
00:28:02,260 --> 00:28:06,040
fully connected
autoencoder, small patches.

565
00:28:06,040 --> 00:28:07,570
The number of
codes is important.

566
00:28:07,570 --> 00:28:09,970
Because if you have
too many codes,

567
00:28:09,970 --> 00:28:11,880
then you are kind
of just throwing

568
00:28:11,880 --> 00:28:13,860
in all kinds of noisy things.

569
00:28:13,860 --> 00:28:17,920
I'll give an example of
why the number of codes

570
00:28:17,920 --> 00:28:19,690
are important through
some examples.

571
00:28:19,690 --> 00:28:22,880
And you have too
little codes, what

572
00:28:22,880 --> 00:28:26,530
you're doing is you're removing
all of the information which

573
00:28:26,530 --> 00:28:27,340
was relevant.

574
00:28:27,340 --> 00:28:29,590
And you're just kind of
averaging them all out.

575
00:28:32,350 --> 00:28:34,750
I'd start with--
so this idea first

576
00:28:34,750 --> 00:28:40,280
was proposed by jukebox,
which did it for music.

577
00:28:40,280 --> 00:28:43,180
So you do the exact same
thing what I talked about

578
00:28:43,180 --> 00:28:46,330
in a slightly different
manner, in a way that, OK,

579
00:28:46,330 --> 00:28:50,640
you cannot learn codes
for longer sequences.

580
00:28:50,640 --> 00:28:52,600
So you, in a way,
learn sequences

581
00:28:52,600 --> 00:28:54,580
which are just moving
slowly and which

582
00:28:54,580 --> 00:28:57,800
are looking at only a
certain amount of audio.

583
00:28:57,800 --> 00:29:01,120
So we kind of encode this
at these discrete levels,

584
00:29:01,120 --> 00:29:03,382
which are basically like--

585
00:29:03,382 --> 00:29:04,840
all of these,
basically, are codes.

586
00:29:04,840 --> 00:29:08,110
So at every point, I
define, OK, this audio

587
00:29:08,110 --> 00:29:10,630
had, perhaps, code number 55.

588
00:29:10,630 --> 00:29:12,880
And in the next level,
perhaps, it had code number 2.

589
00:29:12,880 --> 00:29:16,450
And the very top, perhaps
it had code number 2,000.

590
00:29:16,450 --> 00:29:19,850
So in a way, I'm
discretizing the whole codes.

591
00:29:19,850 --> 00:29:23,500
Now, what I do is I take up
my favorite transform model,

592
00:29:23,500 --> 00:29:26,380
perhaps a causal
autoregressive one,

593
00:29:26,380 --> 00:29:29,290
and I say that, OK,
given these codes,

594
00:29:29,290 --> 00:29:31,510
try to predict what
codes would come next.

595
00:29:31,510 --> 00:29:34,280
And for sure,
transformers can do that.

596
00:29:34,280 --> 00:29:36,850
So I would generate the
codes in the future.

597
00:29:36,850 --> 00:29:39,580
Once I've generated the
codes in the future,

598
00:29:39,580 --> 00:29:41,410
I can say that, OK,
this problem now

599
00:29:41,410 --> 00:29:43,630
is kind of like a
text-to-speech problem, right?

600
00:29:43,630 --> 00:29:45,640
Because I have this
discrete codes.

601
00:29:45,640 --> 00:29:48,700
Text to speech, in a way, is
going from discrete letters

602
00:29:48,700 --> 00:29:50,530
to continuous audio.

603
00:29:50,530 --> 00:29:53,770
So I would throw in the
fanciest, which was WaveNet,

604
00:29:53,770 --> 00:29:55,300
I would just get back the code.

605
00:29:55,300 --> 00:29:58,120
And I would get the
generated audio.

606
00:29:58,120 --> 00:30:02,350
So this was, in a
way, what I described.

607
00:30:02,350 --> 00:30:04,810
That they take up
a continuous audio.

608
00:30:04,810 --> 00:30:08,080
They have these compressed
codes which they encode

609
00:30:08,080 --> 00:30:10,790
using a CNN in this case.

610
00:30:10,790 --> 00:30:12,140
The method doesn't matter.

611
00:30:12,140 --> 00:30:13,720
You can throw in
the fancier stuff,

612
00:30:13,720 --> 00:30:15,940
like embedding or
latent representation

613
00:30:15,940 --> 00:30:18,370
on those continuous code.

614
00:30:18,370 --> 00:30:20,170
You generate the
patterns, which are

615
00:30:20,170 --> 00:30:22,000
what's going to happen
next in the future.

616
00:30:22,000 --> 00:30:25,240
And then you decode back
using a fancy WaveNet

617
00:30:25,240 --> 00:30:27,820
or state-of-the-art model.

618
00:30:27,820 --> 00:30:32,200
So this was what we were
doing for music synthesis.

619
00:30:32,200 --> 00:30:34,468
What we said was--

620
00:30:34,468 --> 00:30:35,260
yeah, this is good.

621
00:30:35,260 --> 00:30:37,150
This can generate
good amount of music.

622
00:30:37,150 --> 00:30:41,140
But what-- can
these models be used

623
00:30:41,140 --> 00:30:45,580
for generating
good representation

624
00:30:45,580 --> 00:30:47,860
of the current audio?

625
00:30:47,860 --> 00:30:51,230
And the goal there was
can language models

626
00:30:51,230 --> 00:30:52,930
learn representation
which can just

627
00:30:52,930 --> 00:30:59,090
encapsulate whatever we are kind
of giving as an input signal?

628
00:30:59,090 --> 00:31:03,310
So in this case, what we
tried after that was kind of--

629
00:31:03,310 --> 00:31:06,790
you kind of do exactly
kind of similar ideas.

630
00:31:06,790 --> 00:31:12,040
But instead of doing on VA-VAE
end-to-end learned encodings,

631
00:31:12,040 --> 00:31:15,040
we just applied vanilla K means
clustering similar to what

632
00:31:15,040 --> 00:31:16,905
I described earlier.

633
00:31:16,905 --> 00:31:18,280
If you do on
spectrogram patches,

634
00:31:18,280 --> 00:31:20,950
so you take up these
spectrograms of audio.

635
00:31:20,950 --> 00:31:24,090
And you just divide them
into very small chunks,

636
00:31:24,090 --> 00:31:28,665
learn autoencoder encodings
for each of those chunks,

637
00:31:28,665 --> 00:31:30,370
run K means clustering.

638
00:31:30,370 --> 00:31:34,210
In this case, let's say,
I'm learning 16 codes,

639
00:31:34,210 --> 00:31:38,380
represent the continuous audio
in terms of the 16 codes,

640
00:31:38,380 --> 00:31:41,890
have a transformer which can
perhaps predict the next code.

641
00:31:41,890 --> 00:31:45,370
And if I keep on getting better
and better at predicting what's

642
00:31:45,370 --> 00:31:48,070
going to happen next,
then in this linear layer,

643
00:31:48,070 --> 00:31:51,370
I should be encapsulating
what's important

644
00:31:51,370 --> 00:31:56,880
or what's a good summary of
what has happened in the past.

645
00:31:56,880 --> 00:32:01,640
So that was kind of our
intuition behind trying this.

646
00:32:01,640 --> 00:32:03,650
And as I explained,
the number of codes

647
00:32:03,650 --> 00:32:05,600
play a very important role.

648
00:32:05,600 --> 00:32:09,140
You can see here, these are just
two piano notes switching one

649
00:32:09,140 --> 00:32:10,170
after the other.

650
00:32:10,170 --> 00:32:12,140
If I just have 16
number of codes,

651
00:32:12,140 --> 00:32:15,590
it just happens to
have just a single line

652
00:32:15,590 --> 00:32:18,650
of encoding, a single code
assigned to all of this.

653
00:32:18,650 --> 00:32:21,080
Whereas, if I'm
assigning more codes,

654
00:32:21,080 --> 00:32:23,630
then it becomes kind of like
a fine-grained prediction,

655
00:32:23,630 --> 00:32:29,360
where I'm actually able to get
what the individual notes are.

656
00:32:29,360 --> 00:32:32,840
Recently, Facebook also
said, OK, they just

657
00:32:32,840 --> 00:32:35,300
had a different name to
the whole thing, which

658
00:32:35,300 --> 00:32:40,100
is we can just call this
as TextLess NLP also.

659
00:32:40,100 --> 00:32:42,470
In the sense that,
OK, you can do an LP

660
00:32:42,470 --> 00:32:44,780
without having access to text.

661
00:32:44,780 --> 00:32:46,372
But the idea is
very, very similar.

662
00:32:46,372 --> 00:32:48,830
You have an encoder, which is
exactly similar to, say, what

663
00:32:48,830 --> 00:32:52,010
openAPI was using, we
have a VQ-VAE, wav2vec,

664
00:32:52,010 --> 00:32:53,330
or whatever you want to do.

665
00:32:53,330 --> 00:32:55,310
You can apply K means
clustering to it.

666
00:32:55,310 --> 00:32:57,170
We apply language models to it.

667
00:32:57,170 --> 00:32:58,940
And instead of a
decoder in WaveNet,

668
00:32:58,940 --> 00:33:00,530
they just have a
decoder which is

669
00:33:00,530 --> 00:33:02,900
a different version of
text to speech, which

670
00:33:02,900 --> 00:33:04,950
is like Tacotron in this case.

671
00:33:04,950 --> 00:33:07,250
So as you can see,
these are all same wine

672
00:33:07,250 --> 00:33:08,360
in very different bottles.

673
00:33:08,360 --> 00:33:12,710
But the core idea is
almost exactly the same.

674
00:33:12,710 --> 00:33:16,040
So this was like-- it created
a huge uproar of like,

675
00:33:16,040 --> 00:33:18,140
oh, this is going to change NLP.

676
00:33:18,140 --> 00:33:21,620
But this is very, very
similar to what people

677
00:33:21,620 --> 00:33:24,690
have been doing in the past.

678
00:33:24,690 --> 00:33:29,280
So I've already
explained what this was.

679
00:33:29,280 --> 00:33:32,450
So in our case, we
just try to predict

680
00:33:32,450 --> 00:33:35,450
what's going to happen next
given the previous context

681
00:33:35,450 --> 00:33:37,490
and use that
representation similar

682
00:33:37,490 --> 00:33:41,600
to every single one-shot
learning or zero-shot

683
00:33:41,600 --> 00:33:44,630
learning based method.

684
00:33:44,630 --> 00:33:47,905
I also explain why the number
of codes are important.

685
00:33:47,905 --> 00:33:49,280
If you are too
small, then you're

686
00:33:49,280 --> 00:33:50,863
just throwing up a
lot of information.

687
00:33:50,863 --> 00:33:55,340
If you have too large,
then you don't put in--

688
00:33:55,340 --> 00:33:59,240
it is no longer robust to noise.

689
00:33:59,240 --> 00:34:00,650
So this was our setup.

690
00:34:00,650 --> 00:34:03,320
And before I jump
in, I should add

691
00:34:03,320 --> 00:34:07,280
one of the tweets
which I saw from one

692
00:34:07,280 --> 00:34:08,780
of the most
prominent researchers

693
00:34:08,780 --> 00:34:11,239
at DeepMind, which is
basically a lot of times,

694
00:34:11,239 --> 00:34:13,639
it is very, very easy
to bump up numbers.

695
00:34:13,639 --> 00:34:18,889
I can have these details just
not present in my paper, which

696
00:34:18,889 --> 00:34:22,250
actually helps a lot in terms
of improving the performance.

697
00:34:22,250 --> 00:34:25,790
And kind of sometimes
don't take into account

698
00:34:25,790 --> 00:34:29,150
what the actual model
is incorporating

699
00:34:29,150 --> 00:34:32,030
or what model is
contributing versus what

700
00:34:32,030 --> 00:34:35,030
the actual these tricks for
training are incorporating.

701
00:34:35,030 --> 00:34:39,020
So for most of these methods,
what we try to see is we

702
00:34:39,020 --> 00:34:42,530
try to keep almost exactly
the same approach, no data

703
00:34:42,530 --> 00:34:44,570
augmentation, no
fancy label smoothing

704
00:34:44,570 --> 00:34:48,380
or moving average of weights
or decay or whatever.

705
00:34:48,380 --> 00:34:51,380
You just have
similar based recipes

706
00:34:51,380 --> 00:34:55,520
to see how well we are doing.

707
00:34:55,520 --> 00:34:57,920
For this case, the
goal was to say

708
00:34:57,920 --> 00:35:00,950
that how well our models do
with respect to this purely

709
00:35:00,950 --> 00:35:02,960
supervised approach,
and how well it

710
00:35:02,960 --> 00:35:06,780
does with respect to a
similar unsupervised approach.

711
00:35:06,780 --> 00:35:09,320
So in the first case, the
model and all of the weights

712
00:35:09,320 --> 00:35:11,195
have access to all of
the labels, which is

713
00:35:11,195 --> 00:35:14,133
shown just as VGG supervised.

714
00:35:14,133 --> 00:35:16,550
Which is basically you take
up an audio understanding data

715
00:35:16,550 --> 00:35:17,050
set.

716
00:35:17,050 --> 00:35:21,410
And you see how well you are
doing on accuracy metrics.

717
00:35:21,410 --> 00:35:22,580
So that was the first one.

718
00:35:22,580 --> 00:35:24,532
And the second one,
we applied SimCLR,

719
00:35:24,532 --> 00:35:26,990
which was proposed by Geoff
Hinton in which you can take up

720
00:35:26,990 --> 00:35:30,440
these multiple augmentations
of the same input.

721
00:35:30,440 --> 00:35:31,990
You can have patches removed.

722
00:35:31,990 --> 00:35:32,990
You can blur the signal.

723
00:35:32,990 --> 00:35:34,820
You can flip the signal.

724
00:35:34,820 --> 00:35:36,890
You learn embedding
onto the last layer

725
00:35:36,890 --> 00:35:38,930
without access to the
labels, and then just

726
00:35:38,930 --> 00:35:41,930
have a linear head to
predict what's happening.

727
00:35:41,930 --> 00:35:45,053
By using that, we
got a 55% accuracy.

728
00:35:45,053 --> 00:35:46,970
You do the exact same
thing with transformers.

729
00:35:46,970 --> 00:35:48,500
You don't have access to labels.

730
00:35:48,500 --> 00:35:51,650
You just run them while
just predict the next code.

731
00:35:51,650 --> 00:35:54,500
You take the linear layer,
apply the same linear head,

732
00:35:54,500 --> 00:35:57,110
and try to predict
what's happening inside.

733
00:35:57,110 --> 00:35:59,390
And with that, we
got 60% accuracy.

734
00:35:59,390 --> 00:36:01,130
So even though the
results are not good,

735
00:36:01,130 --> 00:36:05,040
but the fact is the
neural networks actually

736
00:36:05,040 --> 00:36:09,390
are very, very good at
getting better and better

737
00:36:09,390 --> 00:36:11,400
with training of
huge amounts of data.

738
00:36:11,400 --> 00:36:14,760
So there's still a 10% gap
between the purely supervised

739
00:36:14,760 --> 00:36:17,130
and purely unsupervised.

740
00:36:17,130 --> 00:36:19,050
But in that, that's
going to improve

741
00:36:19,050 --> 00:36:21,930
with training a lot of
data to these models,

742
00:36:21,930 --> 00:36:25,270
because it doesn't have access
to any label that's present.

743
00:36:25,270 --> 00:36:28,590
So this is a famous paper by
Dan Ellis and Nelson Morgan

744
00:36:28,590 --> 00:36:30,300
at Berkeley, in
which they actually

745
00:36:30,300 --> 00:36:36,120
showed, way back in 1999,
as to why size matters

746
00:36:36,120 --> 00:36:37,680
for deep neural
networks, and also

747
00:36:37,680 --> 00:36:41,050
the number of data
points, which is present.

748
00:36:41,050 --> 00:36:44,070
So as they kept on increasing
the size of the data

749
00:36:44,070 --> 00:36:45,840
set and the
parameters, they kept

750
00:36:45,840 --> 00:36:47,910
on getting lower and
lower error rates.

751
00:36:47,910 --> 00:36:51,372
And this has been true
across any of the data set.

752
00:36:51,372 --> 00:36:52,830
And that's why the
whole excitement

753
00:36:52,830 --> 00:36:56,670
is about unsupervised learning.

754
00:36:56,670 --> 00:36:59,040
So this was, in a way,
a flavor of how can we

755
00:36:59,040 --> 00:37:02,940
do language modeling and
unsupervised learning on audio

756
00:37:02,940 --> 00:37:05,190
for continuous signals.

757
00:37:05,190 --> 00:37:07,260
For the third subplot,
I'll just quickly

758
00:37:07,260 --> 00:37:11,400
mention ideas which are very
similar to what you would have

759
00:37:11,400 --> 00:37:16,200
seen in vision transformers,
but with the caveat that how can

760
00:37:16,200 --> 00:37:18,870
we use some sort of
signal processing

761
00:37:18,870 --> 00:37:21,840
to improve these
performance even further.

762
00:37:21,840 --> 00:37:24,510
So the basic approach still
remains the same exactly

763
00:37:24,510 --> 00:37:28,110
as what you would have seen
in vision transformers.

764
00:37:28,110 --> 00:37:33,000
You have a signal of interest,
which you want to classify.

765
00:37:33,000 --> 00:37:36,330
Here, they are waveform
in sort of images.

766
00:37:36,330 --> 00:37:41,160
The goal is to predict what's
then inside of it, right?

767
00:37:41,160 --> 00:37:43,470
And also, we don't
have any convolutions.

768
00:37:43,470 --> 00:37:46,740
We don't have any other tricks
which we were using before.

769
00:37:46,740 --> 00:37:49,800
All we have to do is they
can-- transformers themselves

770
00:37:49,800 --> 00:37:52,900
solve this particular problem.

771
00:37:52,900 --> 00:37:54,810
So for the data set--

772
00:37:54,810 --> 00:37:57,240
and the whole setup
was still the same.

773
00:37:57,240 --> 00:38:03,000
No data augmentation and no
other forms of these tricks.

774
00:38:03,000 --> 00:38:05,460
You are given 40,000
snippets for training

775
00:38:05,460 --> 00:38:08,100
and 10,000 for validation.

776
00:38:08,100 --> 00:38:10,890
Our job is to predict
as good as possible

777
00:38:10,890 --> 00:38:13,500
as to what's there in the audio.

778
00:38:13,500 --> 00:38:17,160
This problem is very similar
to the sound which you heard

779
00:38:17,160 --> 00:38:18,810
and the video which you saw.

780
00:38:18,810 --> 00:38:20,862
That given this batch,
you are to predict--

781
00:38:20,862 --> 00:38:22,320
given a spectrogram
patch, you have

782
00:38:22,320 --> 00:38:24,180
to predict what's
there inside of it.

783
00:38:27,130 --> 00:38:32,840
We kind of do one step
further than what's

784
00:38:32,840 --> 00:38:34,940
just a simple transform model.

785
00:38:34,940 --> 00:38:36,350
In the sense that
we tried to see

786
00:38:36,350 --> 00:38:40,820
whether some sort of
hierarchy over transform

787
00:38:40,820 --> 00:38:43,920
embeddings would help
us in any manner.

788
00:38:43,920 --> 00:38:47,240
So for that, we used
wavelet decomposition

789
00:38:47,240 --> 00:38:50,720
on the intermediate
transformer embeddings.

790
00:38:50,720 --> 00:38:55,310
So what is a wavelet
decomposition?

791
00:38:55,310 --> 00:38:58,280
In very naive terms,
it can be a way

792
00:38:58,280 --> 00:39:02,780
of decomposing the
intermediate embeddings

793
00:39:02,780 --> 00:39:06,860
into other intermediate
embedding in the sense

794
00:39:06,860 --> 00:39:09,170
that we are kind of
putting these highways

795
00:39:09,170 --> 00:39:11,510
of some embeddings are
moving very slowly,

796
00:39:11,510 --> 00:39:13,610
and some embeddings
are moving very fast,

797
00:39:13,610 --> 00:39:15,650
and some embeddings
are retained exactly

798
00:39:15,650 --> 00:39:19,520
at the rate of what the
original signal was.

799
00:39:19,520 --> 00:39:20,720
And why this is important?

800
00:39:20,720 --> 00:39:24,050
Because you can think about that
at every intermediate state,

801
00:39:24,050 --> 00:39:27,434
you are, in a way, learning some
sort of hierarchy in the model.

802
00:39:27,434 --> 00:39:35,120
So if I look at what we do
with the wavelet decomposition

803
00:39:35,120 --> 00:39:39,230
before and after, let's say
you had time across this

804
00:39:39,230 --> 00:39:41,660
and you had the embedding
size across this.

805
00:39:41,660 --> 00:39:45,320
And this whole patch
was your output

806
00:39:45,320 --> 00:39:48,620
of say, the nth layer
of the transformer.

807
00:39:48,620 --> 00:39:52,370
What I say now is,
OK, I would just

808
00:39:52,370 --> 00:39:56,060
have a mapping from this to
the mapping of my interest

809
00:39:56,060 --> 00:40:00,260
using wavelet decomposition, in
which for half of the samples,

810
00:40:00,260 --> 00:40:03,440
I just created the exact
same embedding as what was

811
00:40:03,440 --> 00:40:05,900
known by the transformer model.

812
00:40:05,900 --> 00:40:09,090
In the next half, I would
start combining two at a time.

813
00:40:09,090 --> 00:40:12,350
So in a way, I'm learning this
sort of like a tree structure

814
00:40:12,350 --> 00:40:16,250
within a single layer of
the transformer embedding.

815
00:40:16,250 --> 00:40:20,990
And for now, the wavelet or
the basis function which I use

816
00:40:20,990 --> 00:40:22,220
is simple averaging.

817
00:40:22,220 --> 00:40:24,740
So let's say I have from
all of the embedding

818
00:40:24,740 --> 00:40:31,027
layers in between, I just need
to have one embedding which

819
00:40:31,027 --> 00:40:33,110
is not moving at all, which
is just representative

820
00:40:33,110 --> 00:40:39,410
of whatever is there of the
whole latent space in that nth

821
00:40:39,410 --> 00:40:40,190
layer.

822
00:40:40,190 --> 00:40:44,180
Then in the next layer, I
would just use two at a time.

823
00:40:44,180 --> 00:40:46,610
And then I would
use four at a time.

824
00:40:46,610 --> 00:40:50,360
Then I reach the exact
resolution as what I had.

825
00:40:50,360 --> 00:40:53,650
Doing this operation doesn't
add any parameters whatsoever.

826
00:40:53,650 --> 00:40:56,330
You're just defining what
your basis function would be

827
00:40:56,330 --> 00:40:58,070
or what your wavelet
function would be.

828
00:40:58,070 --> 00:41:00,260
In this case, it
is a Haar wavelet.

829
00:41:00,260 --> 00:41:01,760
And I start combining them.

830
00:41:01,760 --> 00:41:05,990
And I kind of learn a
hierarchy at every single layer

831
00:41:05,990 --> 00:41:08,330
of the transformers.

832
00:41:08,330 --> 00:41:12,830
And this kind of improved our
performance significantly as

833
00:41:12,830 --> 00:41:15,110
compared to not using
them, with addition

834
00:41:15,110 --> 00:41:17,810
of no extra parameters.

835
00:41:17,810 --> 00:41:20,790
And I've come to the
results later also.

836
00:41:20,790 --> 00:41:23,900
So this is how the whole
approach looks like.

837
00:41:23,900 --> 00:41:25,700
You have a frontend.

838
00:41:25,700 --> 00:41:28,520
The frontend is
basically a single layer

839
00:41:28,520 --> 00:41:33,290
of 2,000 neurons followed by
a dense layer of 64 neurons,

840
00:41:33,290 --> 00:41:37,348
which is just to make
sure to conform it

841
00:41:37,348 --> 00:41:39,140
to the intermediate
transformer embeddings.

842
00:41:39,140 --> 00:41:40,970
Let's say if for
the transformers

843
00:41:40,970 --> 00:41:43,760
I define the embedding
size to be 64, then

844
00:41:43,760 --> 00:41:47,150
that's their dimension
which I'm mapping them to.

845
00:41:47,150 --> 00:41:51,290
So I take a broad waveform, I
patch it in very small patches,

846
00:41:51,290 --> 00:41:54,620
similar to how you do
in vision transformers.

847
00:41:54,620 --> 00:41:57,350
I would just have a single
layer of 2,000 neurons followed

848
00:41:57,350 --> 00:42:00,590
by a dense layer of 64
neurons, with the hope

849
00:42:00,590 --> 00:42:03,260
that the first layer kind of
is learning a Fourier basis

850
00:42:03,260 --> 00:42:06,350
function, which
should be adaptable

851
00:42:06,350 --> 00:42:08,210
according to what I'm learning.

852
00:42:08,210 --> 00:42:11,570
After that, I keep on doing
this over and over again.

853
00:42:11,570 --> 00:42:15,430
I don't have a classification
head or anything like that.

854
00:42:15,430 --> 00:42:20,270
I keep on adding multiple stacks
of transformers after that.

855
00:42:23,010 --> 00:42:25,980
And then I have two
approaches of what I

856
00:42:25,980 --> 00:42:28,980
can do in terms of adaptation.

857
00:42:28,980 --> 00:42:31,080
I can do average
pooling across time

858
00:42:31,080 --> 00:42:32,760
of these intermediate
embeddings.

859
00:42:32,760 --> 00:42:34,470
Because that is
very-- the idea is

860
00:42:34,470 --> 00:42:36,985
very similar to what we
do in classical version.

861
00:42:36,985 --> 00:42:38,610
That each of the
embeddings are looking

862
00:42:38,610 --> 00:42:44,250
at much, much broader output
in the subsequent layers.

863
00:42:44,250 --> 00:42:47,260
Or I could do a
wavelet decomposition.

864
00:42:47,260 --> 00:42:50,130
So what I do is that I take
all of these embeddings,

865
00:42:50,130 --> 00:42:51,470
and I define these highways.

866
00:42:51,470 --> 00:42:53,220
So some of the embeddings
are moving fast.

867
00:42:53,220 --> 00:42:54,750
Some of them are
moving very slow.

868
00:42:54,750 --> 00:42:57,660
And some are retained at the
exact same resolution as what

869
00:42:57,660 --> 00:42:59,700
the transformer is learning.

870
00:42:59,700 --> 00:43:02,190
And then I keep doing
this over and over again.

871
00:43:02,190 --> 00:43:03,720
I have a dense layer.

872
00:43:03,720 --> 00:43:06,570
I have my softmax
or sigmoid, whatever

873
00:43:06,570 --> 00:43:08,640
is my classification head.

874
00:43:08,640 --> 00:43:12,420
So this is kind of what
the approach looks like.

875
00:43:12,420 --> 00:43:17,760
We compare it with all of
the traditional vision based

876
00:43:17,760 --> 00:43:18,330
architecture.

877
00:43:18,330 --> 00:43:21,090
So the vision based models
have been very good.

878
00:43:21,090 --> 00:43:24,680
And the performance have been
similar in understanding audio

879
00:43:24,680 --> 00:43:25,530
also.

880
00:43:25,530 --> 00:43:27,885
So we compare all of
those models in terms

881
00:43:27,885 --> 00:43:29,670
of mean average position.

882
00:43:29,670 --> 00:43:32,490
And we see that even the tiniest
models of transformers were

883
00:43:32,490 --> 00:43:35,700
just surpassing all of the
state-of-the-art CNN models.

884
00:43:35,700 --> 00:43:37,960
Which was a very good sign.

885
00:43:37,960 --> 00:43:41,250
Then we started to bump up, OK,
the larger model should keep

886
00:43:41,250 --> 00:43:42,600
on improving the performance.

887
00:43:42,600 --> 00:43:46,140
And with the multiscale
models, as well as with

888
00:43:46,140 --> 00:43:50,870
the pooling layers, they improve
the performance even further,

889
00:43:50,870 --> 00:43:52,560
which was kind of
very surprising to us,

890
00:43:52,560 --> 00:43:55,000
because the number of
parameters are very small.

891
00:43:55,000 --> 00:43:56,490
These are very
tiny architectures,

892
00:43:56,490 --> 00:43:59,070
yet they are surpassing things
like even DenseNet, which

893
00:43:59,070 --> 00:44:03,930
are huge models with a lot
of millions of parameters.

894
00:44:03,930 --> 00:44:08,280
So after that, we said, and
I'm going to conclude quickly,

895
00:44:08,280 --> 00:44:12,210
after that we said that, OK,
this is looking pretty cool.

896
00:44:12,210 --> 00:44:16,380
What actually is the transformer
or the first layer learning

897
00:44:16,380 --> 00:44:17,500
learning, right?

898
00:44:17,500 --> 00:44:22,500
So in order to make
this plot, what we said

899
00:44:22,500 --> 00:44:27,600
was, OK, if you were to take
a classic Fourier transform,

900
00:44:27,600 --> 00:44:33,030
then if my-- this axis is
kind of like frequency.

901
00:44:33,030 --> 00:44:34,560
This axis is the
number of filters,

902
00:44:34,560 --> 00:44:36,720
and this axis is
of the frequency.

903
00:44:36,720 --> 00:44:41,160
Then in a way, it
should be connecting all

904
00:44:41,160 --> 00:44:43,540
of the points in a linear line.

905
00:44:43,540 --> 00:44:45,960
And this is akin to the
number of points in FFT.

906
00:44:45,960 --> 00:44:48,330
So how many points
I'm defining here,

907
00:44:48,330 --> 00:44:50,610
if I'm defining
2,000 points here,

908
00:44:50,610 --> 00:44:56,100
then I would have 2,048
sinusoidal basis functions,

909
00:44:56,100 --> 00:44:58,200
which are going from lower
frequency to the most

910
00:44:58,200 --> 00:45:00,240
highest frequency.

911
00:45:00,240 --> 00:45:02,430
We said, OK, we'll do the
exact same thing, but now

912
00:45:02,430 --> 00:45:03,610
with filters.

913
00:45:03,610 --> 00:45:06,840
So we have a
frequency along y-axis

914
00:45:06,840 --> 00:45:09,460
and the number of
points in my x-axis.

915
00:45:09,460 --> 00:45:11,580
And if it was a classic
Fourier transform, then

916
00:45:11,580 --> 00:45:15,640
it would be connecting
right as a linear line.

917
00:45:15,640 --> 00:45:19,680
But what we did was we
take up the frontend which

918
00:45:19,680 --> 00:45:22,800
is known by transformer,
take its Fourier transform,

919
00:45:22,800 --> 00:45:26,070
sort according to its center
frequency as to what frequency

920
00:45:26,070 --> 00:45:30,030
is activating the most, and
then keep on stacking them.

921
00:45:30,030 --> 00:45:31,980
When we did this
for two problems,

922
00:45:31,980 --> 00:45:35,280
we saw that we are kind of
learning a different time

923
00:45:35,280 --> 00:45:36,960
frequency
representation, which is

924
00:45:36,960 --> 00:45:38,847
specific to a
particular problem.

925
00:45:38,847 --> 00:45:40,680
So if I'm trying to
understand what's there,

926
00:45:40,680 --> 00:45:43,152
the content of the audio
by learned representation,

927
00:45:43,152 --> 00:45:45,360
which is very different than
Fourier transform, which

928
00:45:45,360 --> 00:45:46,943
would have been a
straight line, which

929
00:45:46,943 --> 00:45:51,640
is kind of like a curved
exponential line like this.

930
00:45:51,640 --> 00:45:54,510
And if I do a polyphonic
pitch estimation,

931
00:45:54,510 --> 00:45:57,340
I learn a very
different frontend,

932
00:45:57,340 --> 00:46:00,010
which is adapting to
that particular problem.

933
00:46:00,010 --> 00:46:05,520
So this was very exciting to
us, because making computers

934
00:46:05,520 --> 00:46:08,220
hear in a way in which they're
adapting their ears according

935
00:46:08,220 --> 00:46:12,390
to a particular problem
is a very cool idea.

936
00:46:12,390 --> 00:46:15,180
Second thing is we actually
saw each of the filters

937
00:46:15,180 --> 00:46:17,250
as to what they were doing.

938
00:46:17,250 --> 00:46:20,940
And these are basically just
like single slices like this.

939
00:46:20,940 --> 00:46:23,130
So this is what
we would have been

940
00:46:23,130 --> 00:46:25,360
learned as a frontend neuron.

941
00:46:25,360 --> 00:46:27,810
So we take up each of the
neurons, and we just plot them.

942
00:46:27,810 --> 00:46:29,760
And for plotting
this, we basically

943
00:46:29,760 --> 00:46:32,730
take its Fourier transform
and then sort them

944
00:46:32,730 --> 00:46:35,173
according to where the
center frequency is.

945
00:46:35,173 --> 00:46:37,590
When we just saw the neurons
as to what they were learning

946
00:46:37,590 --> 00:46:41,580
in the front end, we saw that
it is learning properties which

947
00:46:41,580 --> 00:46:46,350
are very, very closely matching
with the traditional signal

948
00:46:46,350 --> 00:46:46,930
processing.

949
00:46:46,930 --> 00:46:49,347
So you would have something
like an onset detector learned

950
00:46:49,347 --> 00:46:50,680
right here.

951
00:46:50,680 --> 00:46:52,180
You're learning
windowing functions.

952
00:46:52,180 --> 00:46:55,410
So in a way, it is learning
to have a kernel which

953
00:46:55,410 --> 00:46:58,333
is best for a time frequency
representation for people who

954
00:46:58,333 --> 00:47:00,000
have been using signal
processing, which

955
00:47:00,000 --> 00:47:03,030
is like a Hamming
or Hanning window.

956
00:47:03,030 --> 00:47:04,740
We are learning
these pure sinusoids,

957
00:47:04,740 --> 00:47:07,380
which are responsible
for activating

958
00:47:07,380 --> 00:47:08,800
a particular frequency.

959
00:47:08,800 --> 00:47:10,530
So you can see the
richness as compared

960
00:47:10,530 --> 00:47:14,430
to having a fixed, purely
sinusoidal basis function

961
00:47:14,430 --> 00:47:16,300
right here.

962
00:47:16,300 --> 00:47:19,980
So this was what we had done.

963
00:47:19,980 --> 00:47:23,250
And then to share
the final thoughts,

964
00:47:23,250 --> 00:47:25,530
I'll conclude by saying
that, OK, transformers

965
00:47:25,530 --> 00:47:27,870
are proving to be a major
advancement in AI research

966
00:47:27,870 --> 00:47:30,000
across the fields.

967
00:47:30,000 --> 00:47:34,290
And it seems like they're
solving everything for now.

968
00:47:34,290 --> 00:47:36,000
And hopefully, this
is not the end.

969
00:47:36,000 --> 00:47:38,490
And we should keep an
eye out, and something

970
00:47:38,490 --> 00:47:41,130
which would change and
have an impact which

971
00:47:41,130 --> 00:47:44,370
is more than what
transformers have put.

972
00:47:44,370 --> 00:47:47,730
And who knows what's
going to come next.

973
00:47:47,730 --> 00:47:49,710
Yeah, so by that,
I'll just conclude.

974
00:47:49,710 --> 00:47:53,160
And I'll be happy
to take questions.

975
00:47:53,160 --> 00:47:54,120
Thank you, Prateek.

976
00:47:54,120 --> 00:47:55,860
That was a really good talk.

977
00:47:55,860 --> 00:47:58,740
And you provided some
really good insights

978
00:47:58,740 --> 00:48:02,430
about how transformers
work on the audio case.

979
00:48:02,430 --> 00:48:04,980
And yeah, thank you for that.

980
00:48:04,980 --> 00:48:10,260
And now I would invite questions
from the class students.

981
